WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.150
- By now we've all seen what happened

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:04.810
at the Capitol riots on January 6th.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:07.210
And we now have a pretty good idea

00:00:07.210 --> 00:00:09.470
of what precipitated those events.

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.560
The house is moving
forward with impeachment

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.810
and the articles of
impeachment are likely to pass.

00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:16.060
Though, at this point
it's unclear at what point

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:18.410
the Senate will take up
the articles of impeachment

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.690
and actually try the President.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:20.700
And that could last well

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.610
into President elect Biden's first term.

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:26.260
But what we do know is
that based on the drafts

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.470
of the impeachment articles
that we've already seen

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.350
it looks like article number one

00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:34.060
we'll be charging President
Trump with incitement to riot.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:36.840
Now incitement to riot is an actual crime.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.680
But it also has a
colloquial meaning as well.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.750
So the question is, is
President Trump guilty

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:44.753
of an incitement to riot?

00:00:44.753 --> 00:00:47.336
(upbeat music)

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.390
Hey LegalEagles, it's time to think

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:51.930
like a First Amendment lawyer.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.220
Because President Trump is
facing serious allegations.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.430
Specifically with respect to incitement

00:00:57.430 --> 00:01:00.150
to starting or fomenting a riot.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:03.150
Now incitement has several
different meanings.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.110
Incitement is actually
a limit on free speech.

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:07.580
It's something that it's not protected

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:08.950
by the First Amendment.

00:01:08.950 --> 00:01:11.410
And incitement is also an actual crime,

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:14.730
generally codified in
every state in the country

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:18.150
and in a federal law and
the district of Columbia.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.530
And those criminal laws
tend to hew pretty closely

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:24.950
to the constitutional limit as
defined by the Supreme court.

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:28.310
And of course, incitement
is also a moral judgment

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.850
that is apart from both its constitutional

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:32.520
and criminal meaning.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.040
But let's take a step back

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.180
because when most people
talk about incitement

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.040
they're talking about its
basis in First Amendment law.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.710
And as you probably know,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.010
the First Amendment protections

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:44.120
extend to individual and collective speech

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.560
as one court put it, that are in pursuit

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.960
of a wide variety of political,

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:51.680
social, economic, educational,
religious, and cultural ends.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:53.490
The Supreme court interpreting
the First Amendment

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:54.680
generally protects free speech

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:58.300
unless it falls within one
of a few narrow categories

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.780
of unprotected speech.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:02.120
Things that we define as true threats

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.490
or sometimes incitement.

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:06.170
The court applies different
levels of scrutiny

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:07.370
to different kinds of speech.

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.220
And when the speeches of a
political and ideological nature

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:14.080
the court considers it to be
a core of the First Amendment.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:15.960
You can imagine that
arguably political speech

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.650
receives the most protections

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:19.190
and the most benefit of the doubt

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.820
when you're analyzing it
under the First Amendment.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.890
And political speech almost always receive

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:25.890
the highest level of
scrutiny by the courts.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.240
In other words, the court
is almost always trying

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:31.250
to bend over backwards to
protect political speech.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:33.050
So what is incitement?

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.030
This category of speech
that is not protected.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:38.970
Well in the landmark case
of Brandenburg versus Ohio,

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.390
the Supreme court held
that the First Amendment

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:42.550
protects speakers who
advocate the use of force

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:46.060
or violating the law
except where such advocacy

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.530
is directed to inciting or
producing imminent lawless action

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:53.020
and is likely to incite
or produce such action.

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:54.590
That's the key test.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.980
The defendant in that case
was Clarence Brandenburg

00:02:56.980 --> 00:02:59.440
who addressed a gathering
of Ku Klux Klan members

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:02.820
in a field in Hamilton
County, Ohio in 1969.

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.530
A journalist recorded Brandenburg

00:03:04.530 --> 00:03:06.640
saying that he was concerned
the federal government

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.320
was going to suppress
the White Caucasian race.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.600
He made derogatory statements

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:12.360
against black people and Jewish people.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.180
He said that the Klan may
need to get quote Revengeance

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.310
on the federal government

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:17.500
if it continued doing things

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:19.770
that he viewed as racist
against white people.

00:03:19.770 --> 00:03:21.410
And Brandenburg said that Klan members

00:03:21.410 --> 00:03:24.620
were preparing a march on
Washington DC for July 4th.

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:27.440
Police arrested Brandenburg
for violating an Ohio law

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.270
which made it a crime to quote
advocate the duty, necessity

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.160
or propriety of crime, sabotage

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.890
or unlawful methods of terrorism

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.980
as a means of accomplishing
industrial or political reform.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.930
And he was convicted at
the trial court level.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.770
Well, Brandenburg fought his conviction

00:03:41.770 --> 00:03:43.070
all the way to the Supreme court

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:46.750
where he argued that the Ohio
law was unconstitutional.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:48.660
At the time of Brandenburg conviction,

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:51.270
the court had been using the
clear and present danger test

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:54.110
to determine when speech
constituted incitement.

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.370
The Supreme court unanimously

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:57.510
actually overturned
Brandenburg's conviction

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:00.330
and issued a new test for
all future restrictions

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.280
of speech on the basis of incitement.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.100
And that test is what
we just talked about.

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.310
It's the Imminent Danger Test.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.010
In other words the Imminent Danger Test

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.680
is speech that's advocating violence

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.500
and can only be punished
where quote such advocacy

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:15.560
is directed to inciting or
producing imminent lawless action

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.860
and is likely to incite
or produce such action.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.420
And in that case, according
to the Supreme court,

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.580
Brandenburg speech did
not invite his followers

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:25.930
to do anything except
march on Washington DC

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:26.763
at a later date.

00:04:26.763 --> 00:04:30.100
Which you can imagine is
generally protected speech.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.900
Now the Supreme court continued to develop

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.300
the imminent lawless action test

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:34.900
in a case called Hess versus Indiana,

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:36.440
where it overturned the conviction

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:39.340
of an anti-war demonstrator
in Bloomington Indiana.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:41.180
There the defendant Gregory Hess

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.377
stood on a curve and yelled,

00:04:42.377 --> 00:04:44.930
"We'll take the street again or later."

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.060
Now I'm not going to bleep that out

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:48.500
because that is the
speech that's involved.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:49.830
And you can't understand these cases

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:53.380
unless you know what's actually
at issue in these cases.

00:04:53.380 --> 00:04:55.170
But the court said that Hess's speech

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:58.240
could reasonably be construed
in a number of different ways.

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.550
Maybe he was advocating
illegal activity later

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.020
but certainly not imminently.

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:04.490
Or maybe he was trying to
tell the crowd to stand down.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.440
In either case, his arrest infringed

00:05:06.440 --> 00:05:09.130
on his First Amendment
right to speak his mind.

00:05:09.130 --> 00:05:11.400
Now the Brandenburg test
was also not satisfied

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.420
in a 1982 case involving
the NAACP's practice

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:15.970
of writing down the names of people

00:05:15.970 --> 00:05:19.110
who violated boycotts of
certain white businesses.

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:21.110
The organization would read the names

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:24.510
of the people who broke the
boycotts aloud at meetings.

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.657
And although one of
their members said that,

00:05:26.657 --> 00:05:30.167
"If we catch any of you going
in any of them racist stores,

00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:32.160
"we're going to break your damn neck."

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.320
The court held that the
statement was not a direct threat

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.960
or an incitement to violence.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.590
In that case, there was no evidence

00:05:37.590 --> 00:05:39.180
that the man quote, authorized, ratified

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:42.090
or directly threatened
the acts of violence.

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.110
Now, although there
haven't been a whole lot

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:47.790
of other incitement cases, a
2015 case bears mentioning.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:49.990
In Bible Believers versus Wayne County

00:05:49.990 --> 00:05:52.070
a divided full the handle
of the Sixth Circuit

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:54.950
which is one step below the Supreme court

00:05:54.950 --> 00:05:57.080
ruled that Wayne County,
Michigan officials

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:58.470
violated the First Amendment rights

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:00.090
of a Christian evangelical group

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.500
when they removed them
from an Arab festival.

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:04.090
The police removed the
group to protect them

00:06:04.090 --> 00:06:06.700
from an audience that was
hostile to the group's views.

00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:08.430
The Christian group made statements like,

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.910
Islam is a religion of blood and murder

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.940
or turn or burn, or your
profit is a pedophile.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.420
The evangelical Christians
walked through the angry crowd

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.740
and although they were
the ones being assaulted,

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.190
the cops kicked them out.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:22.450
Punishing them for their speech

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.360
rather than punishing the
crowd for breaking the law.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:26.910
Now, this case was about
whether the First Amendment

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.300
protected unpopular speech.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:29.550
And it clearly does.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.200
But it also touched briefly
on the incitement doctrine.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:33.177
The court emphasized the quote,

00:06:33.177 --> 00:06:34.747
"The hostile reaction of a crowd

00:06:34.747 --> 00:06:38.260
"does not transform protected
speech into incitement."

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:41.650
This is what is often referred
to as a heckler's veto.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.400
And here the words that the speaker said

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:45.150
weren't advocating violence or crime,

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.670
they were expressing an opinion

00:06:46.670 --> 00:06:49.110
that another religion was violent.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.980
And while potentially offensive,

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:53.047
this was still considered
protected speech.

00:06:53.047 --> 00:06:54.800
- And we're not here to cause a problem,

00:06:54.800 --> 00:06:58.780
it's just, when we talk about
Jesus, this is what happens.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:06:59.890
And in a different case,

00:06:59.890 --> 00:07:02.800
the Sixth Circuit relied heavily
on the Bible Believers case

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:04.740
when they had to assess
Trump's culpability

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.490
for assaults that happened
at one of his rallies.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.910
Oh, what's that, President
Trump was accused

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:11.670
of incitement before?

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:12.730
Yes, he was.

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.180
If you thought the Capitol
riots were an isolated incident

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:17.050
you haven't been paying attention.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:19.750
What happened is Donald Trump
was actually sued in 2016

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:21.370
for starting a riot.

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:23.270
On the election trail in 2016,

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:24.690
he held a rally in Louisville.

00:07:24.690 --> 00:07:27.110
The rally was organized by his campaign.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.880
And during that rally, then
candidate Trump said five times

00:07:30.880 --> 00:07:32.960
to have protestors removed.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:34.930
- Get him the hell out, get him out.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:35.860
Don't hurt him.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:37.550
- In response, members of the audience

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:40.330
assaulted, pushed and shoved these people.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:42.500
A Trump supporter punched
one of them in the stomach.

00:07:42.500 --> 00:07:44.800
Two other Trump's supporters
participated in assaults

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.660
and the victims sued the perpetrators,

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:48.480
and they also sued President Trump

00:07:48.480 --> 00:07:51.600
for inciting a riot at his campaign event.

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:53.260
Now Kentucky Law makes it a misdemeanor

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.377
to incite a riot.

00:07:54.377 --> 00:07:56.597
"A person is guilty of inciting to riot

00:07:56.597 --> 00:07:59.247
"when he incites or urges
five or more persons

00:07:59.247 --> 00:08:01.457
"to create or engage in a riot.

00:08:01.457 --> 00:08:03.327
"Riot is defined as a public disturbance

00:08:03.327 --> 00:08:05.407
"involving an assemblage
of five or more persons,

00:08:05.407 --> 00:08:07.207
"which by tumultuous and violent conduct

00:08:07.207 --> 00:08:09.037
"creates grave danger of damage

00:08:09.037 --> 00:08:11.200
"or injury to property or persons."

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:13.570
Now on appeal, the Sixth
Circuit Court of Appeals

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:17.580
ruled that Donald Trump did
not incite a riot by his words.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.680
Trump wasn't telling everyone
to commit a crime, why?

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.380
Because the court reasoned,
he followed his statement

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:25.944
with get him out of
here, but don't hurt him.

00:08:25.944 --> 00:08:27.450
- Don't hurt him.

00:08:27.450 --> 00:08:28.350
- So you could assume

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.820
that he meant that the
protestors should be ejected

00:08:30.820 --> 00:08:33.740
but not that the tumultuous
or violent conduct

00:08:33.740 --> 00:08:36.670
should be used to eject
them and hurt them.

00:08:36.670 --> 00:08:39.780
Though you can see how highly
context dependent this is.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.170
This could also have been interpreted

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:44.420
as a rhetorical hypothesis or antiphrasis

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:47.150
where by raising the
issue, even in the negative

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:48.940
he's actually implying the positive

00:08:48.940 --> 00:08:52.320
to hurt the bystanders and the protestors.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:54.600
Now the Brandenburg
standard is closely tracked

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.060
by the Kentucky statute.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.090
However, the court held

00:08:57.090 --> 00:08:59.320
that even if President Trump's conduct

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.540
incited a riot under Kentucky Law,

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:03.980
the First Amendment would bar the claim.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:05.730
And that's because for speech

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:08.340
to be outside of the protection
of the First Amendment

00:09:08.340 --> 00:09:11.800
it has to actually encourage
violence or lawlessness.

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:13.920
But like the Ohio Klan's men case,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.210
the court found that Trump's speech

00:09:15.210 --> 00:09:18.760
did not encourage violence or
lawlessness, even implicitly.

00:09:18.760 --> 00:09:19.593
The court distinguished

00:09:19.593 --> 00:09:22.080
between speech that tends to
encourage violence generally

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:23.960
and speech that advocated for listeners

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:26.620
to take specific unlawful action.

00:09:26.620 --> 00:09:29.420
And here the court cited the
Hess and Bible Believers cases

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:30.650
to make their point.

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.500
Hess told his audience
what he intended to do,

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:34.550
but he didn't advocate
for them to do anything.

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:36.110
And in the Bible Believers case,

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:37.610
they were expressing their belief

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:39.220
about the Islamic religion,

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:40.940
but that didn't necessarily
give the listeners

00:09:40.940 --> 00:09:43.620
the right to break the law
because they were mad about it.

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:45.380
And under First Amendment law,

00:09:45.380 --> 00:09:47.260
words generally can't be punished

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:50.130
simply because they tend
to evoke a certain response

00:09:50.130 --> 00:09:51.380
in the listener.

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:52.330
So with that in mind,

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.470
let's look at what President
Trump actually said

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:58.430
on January 6th, right
before the Capitol riots.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:00.430
Knowing what we now know about incitement

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:01.630
and the Brandenburg test

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:04.110
let's apply it to
President Trump's speech.

00:10:04.110 --> 00:10:06.900
President Trump used the
word fight more than 20 times

00:10:06.900 --> 00:10:09.400
during his rally speech on January 6th.

00:10:09.400 --> 00:10:10.820
Was that incitement?

00:10:10.820 --> 00:10:12.630
Well, the first question is
what did he mean by fight?

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:14.450
Did he mean the crowd
should actually fight?

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:17.400
Or was it a metaphor that
they need to fight things

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:18.670
in the abstract?

00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:20.680
Consider the statement.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:23.023
- And we fight, we fight like hell,

00:10:23.023 --> 00:10:25.270
and if you don't fight like hell

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:27.440
you're not gonna have a country anymore.

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.670
- This sounds scary in light
of what eventually happened.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:30.960
The storming of the Capitol

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.770
that left at least five people dead.

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:35.570
But he also doesn't say
punch people or break windows

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.030
or fight the police specifically

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:39.870
or even fight Democrats specifically.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.440
He never even said anything like he did

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.680
in Kentucky back in 2016,

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:44.660
get him out of here.

00:10:44.660 --> 00:10:46.140
Instead, he says.

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:49.002
- We're gonna walk down to the Capitol.

00:10:49.002 --> 00:10:51.610
(crowd applauding)

00:10:51.610 --> 00:10:55.410
And we're gonna cheer
on our brave senators

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.920
and congressmen and women.

00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:00.020
- Here again, President Trump
doesn't tell the audience

00:11:00.020 --> 00:11:01.470
to do anything but fight and cheer,

00:11:01.470 --> 00:11:03.820
which can be taken in multiple directions.

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:07.010
In the democracy, in the
context of an election campaign,

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:10.260
it's pretty easy to view this
as fighting to get elected

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:11.160
and not giving up.

00:11:11.160 --> 00:11:15.070
Regardless of what you think
of whether the election in 2020

00:11:15.070 --> 00:11:16.310
was run perfectly well,

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.500
or if it was fraught with fraud,

00:11:18.500 --> 00:11:21.160
you can imagine that this is
sort of an abstract statement

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.230
about what he wants to be accomplished.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:26.240
And here the Congress was actually meeting

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.240
to finalize the election results.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:30.290
But what about this other phrase?

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:33.290
- We have come to demand that Congress

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.060
do the right thing and
only count the electors

00:11:37.060 --> 00:11:41.313
who have been lawfully
slated, lawfully slated.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:44.880
I know that everyone here
will soon be marching

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:47.100
over to the Capitol building

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:51.700
to peacefully and patriotically
make your voices heard.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:55.300
- It doesn't seem like he's
advocating for specific violence

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.810
or trying to encourage specific
people to break the law.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:11:59.900
At least not based on the explicit text.

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:01.280
Again, context matters.

00:12:01.280 --> 00:12:04.630
And there's certainly lots
of subtext to read into this.

00:12:04.630 --> 00:12:06.360
And at the time the only
person that President Trump

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.660
wanted to explicitly break
the law was Mike Pence.

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:10.493
But on the other hand

00:12:10.493 --> 00:12:11.950
even though President Trump was advocating

00:12:11.950 --> 00:12:14.360
for something to happen
it really couldn't happen.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:15.780
Overturning Biden's election.

00:12:15.780 --> 00:12:17.830
He specifically says
he wants his followers

00:12:17.830 --> 00:12:20.120
to march to the Capitol.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.060
And if we're at least looking
at the literal meaning

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:23.330
of President Trump's words

00:12:23.330 --> 00:12:25.820
he's saying that people
should march to the Capitol.

00:12:25.820 --> 00:12:28.650
Which is a core First Amendment

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:31.220
protected activity, generally speaking.

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:32.360
But what about this?

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:36.510
- But we're going to try
and give our Republicans

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:38.390
the weak ones because the strong ones

00:12:38.390 --> 00:12:40.250
don't need any of our help.

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:44.800
We're going to try and
give them the kind of pride

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.170
and boldness that they need
to take back our country.

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:51.920
So let's walk down Pennsylvania Avenue.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.470
- Here, encouraging people
to have pride and boldness

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:56.670
is probably too vague to imply violence.

00:12:56.670 --> 00:12:59.380
Especially when he also
told them to be peaceful.

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.770
And what it comes down to
is that President Trump

00:13:01.770 --> 00:13:04.430
asked people to march on the Capitol.

00:13:04.430 --> 00:13:06.120
To his audience, President Trump's words

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.340
are probably considered
incredibly authoritative.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:09.850
The President said to go to the Capitol.

00:13:09.850 --> 00:13:11.380
So we're going to go there with him.

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.890
And in fact, that's exactly what happened.

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.830
When President Trump's speech was over

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:18.870
they pretty much all
marched towards the Capitol.

00:13:18.870 --> 00:13:20.950
But still President Trump didn't tell them

00:13:20.950 --> 00:13:24.950
to fight police or to storm the
Capitol or to even trespass.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:27.310
On the other hand, context
is incredibly important.

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:30.010
President Trump started his
speech at roughly 12 noon

00:13:30.010 --> 00:13:31.300
and it wasn't until 12:30

00:13:31.300 --> 00:13:33.270
that the crowds descended on the Capitol.

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:35.000
By about 1:00 p.m.,
the crowds were already

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.350
pushing into the outer
barricades of the Capitol.

00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:39.930
Around 1:10 p.m., President
Trump ends his speech,

00:13:39.930 --> 00:13:41.863
which includes line, we're
going to the Capitol.

00:13:41.863 --> 00:13:46.370
- You are going to walk
down Pennsylvania Avenue.

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:50.160
I love Pennsylvania Avenue and
we're going to the Capitol.

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:51.580
- And if you don't fight,

00:13:51.580 --> 00:13:53.630
you're not going to
have a country anymore.

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.040
Of course his speech was given

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.490
at the White House in The Ellipse

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:59.610
which is about a mile and a
half away from the Capitol.

00:13:59.610 --> 00:14:02.570
So you can imagine that
a court might consider

00:14:02.570 --> 00:14:05.100
that this would be a
reasonable cooling off period.

00:14:05.100 --> 00:14:08.450
That's a pretty far
distance to travel on foot.

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:11.580
In terms of whether you're
inciting a crowd to do something.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.860
But by 1:30, the crowd had moved

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:15.650
from the White House to the Capitol.

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:19.070
And by about 2:15, they had
breached the Capitol itself

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:22.500
and were on the inside with
all of the Congress members.

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:25.120
But things got a lot hairier at 2:24 p.m.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.130
with writers actually inside the Capitol,

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:28.980
actively looking for members of Congress

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.570
and Vice President Pence

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.920
presumably to do him great physical harm.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:34.447
President Trump tweets out,

00:14:34.447 --> 00:14:35.817
"Mike Pence didn't have the courage

00:14:35.817 --> 00:14:36.797
"to do what should have been done

00:14:36.797 --> 00:14:38.747
"to protect our country
and our constitution.

00:14:38.747 --> 00:14:41.467
"Giving States a chance to
certify a corrected set of facts

00:14:41.467 --> 00:14:43.427
"not the fraudulent or inaccurate ones,

00:14:43.427 --> 00:14:45.527
"which they were asked
to previously certify.

00:14:45.527 --> 00:14:47.940
"USA demands the truth."

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:50.870
But then President Trump
follows that up at 2:38 p.m.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.197
stating via tweet,

00:14:52.197 --> 00:14:54.390
"Please support our Capitol
Police and Law Enforcement

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:57.230
they are truly on the side of
our country, stay peaceful.

00:14:57.230 --> 00:14:59.574
Though shortly thereafter at 3:15 p.m.,

00:14:59.574 --> 00:15:01.370
Ashli Babbitt is shot and killed

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:03.667
which is at the same time that
President Trump tweets out,

00:15:03.667 --> 00:15:05.287
"I'm asking for everyone
in the U.S. Capitol

00:15:05.287 --> 00:15:07.067
"to remain peaceful, no violence.

00:15:07.067 --> 00:15:10.157
"Remember we are the party of
law and order, respect the law

00:15:10.157 --> 00:15:12.630
"and our great men and
women in blue, thank you."

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:15.760
Now by 4:05 p.m., President
elect Biden goes on TV

00:15:15.760 --> 00:15:17.460
to demand an end to the siege.

00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:21.987
- It borders on sedition
and it must end now.

00:15:23.210 --> 00:15:25.900
- And by 4:17 p.m.,
President Trump released

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:28.840
his now famous TV
address calling for calm,

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:30.700
which he has later repudiated.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:32.260
- We have to have peace.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:33.640
- So this is a set of facts

00:15:33.640 --> 00:15:35.450
that honestly could go either way.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:37.430
Incitement is a kind of legal unicorn.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:39.970
It's much rarer than RICO or entrapment.

00:15:39.970 --> 00:15:42.810
And almost always
incitement is used to try

00:15:42.810 --> 00:15:44.970
to silence protected speech.

00:15:44.970 --> 00:15:47.100
But this whole situation
comes about as close

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.790
as you'll ever see to a
true case of incitement.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:51.590
It really could go either way.

00:15:51.590 --> 00:15:52.950
But at the same time

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:55.190
we are squarely looking down the barrel

00:15:55.190 --> 00:15:58.450
of generally protected political speech.

00:15:58.450 --> 00:16:00.240
And I think that if a court

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:01.073
especially the Supreme court

00:16:01.073 --> 00:16:03.490
will to look at the actual language here,

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:05.880
I think they are going to
look at this kind of case

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:07.400
with about as much scrutiny

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.600
as they're ever going to apply
and probably give the speaker

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:12.950
of what is arguably political speech

00:16:12.950 --> 00:16:14.510
every benefit of the doubt.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:15.990
Which makes a conviction

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:19.320
for a criminal case of
incitement extremely unlikely.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:20.940
But the question remains

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:22.880
does this mean that President
Trump is off the hook?

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:25.540
Because his words might
not meet the actual

00:16:25.540 --> 00:16:28.830
technical legal standard
for a criminal incitement?

00:16:28.830 --> 00:16:30.380
Well, I'm here to tell you

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:32.190
that, that is merely the
beginning of the story,

00:16:32.190 --> 00:16:33.580
it is not the end of the story.

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:37.240
Because impeachment and
removal is always on the table.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:38.120
I'd covered this before,

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:39.760
so I'll only touch on this briefly.

00:16:39.760 --> 00:16:41.240
The legal standard for impeachment

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.080
is in Article two Section four,

00:16:43.080 --> 00:16:44.690
which says a president can be impeached

00:16:44.690 --> 00:16:48.010
for treason bribery and other
high crimes and misdemeanors.

00:16:48.010 --> 00:16:49.740
As you know, from my past videos

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.860
on President Trump's first impeachment,

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:54.390
sources on how the terms
high crimes and misdemeanors

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:56.040
should be defined are sparse.

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.540
But some legal scholars
like Jonathan Turley

00:16:58.540 --> 00:16:59.910
are adamant that this means

00:16:59.910 --> 00:17:02.180
that Congress can only
impeach and remove a person

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:04.120
if they've committed an actual crime

00:17:04.120 --> 00:17:06.340
as defined in criminal law.

00:17:06.340 --> 00:17:08.000
I think this is absolutely wrong

00:17:08.000 --> 00:17:10.320
in the vast majority
of legal scholars agree

00:17:10.320 --> 00:17:13.520
that an actual technical
legal criminal violation

00:17:13.520 --> 00:17:15.450
is not necessary for impeachment.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:18.190
No matter what political
and Trump supporting flax

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:20.920
like Alan Dershowitz
and Jonathan Turley say.

00:17:20.920 --> 00:17:22.930
Now the founders were concerned
about offenses committed

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:24.210
against the country itself

00:17:24.210 --> 00:17:26.290
by people in high places of authority.

00:17:26.290 --> 00:17:29.990
Therefore the term high crimes
doesn't mean a high felony

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:31.060
like murder or kidnapping.

00:17:31.060 --> 00:17:34.160
Instead the phrase high crime
implies an offense by a person

00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:35.410
in a high place of authority.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:37.420
Just like it did in England.

00:17:37.420 --> 00:17:38.550
The constitution also says

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:40.780
a public official can be
impeached for a misdemeanor.

00:17:40.780 --> 00:17:41.900
Now in American criminal law

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:43.630
a misdemeanor is a crime punishable

00:17:43.630 --> 00:17:45.220
by less than one year in jail.

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:47.400
But misdemeanors are
crimes like petty theft,

00:17:47.400 --> 00:17:48.310
possession of marijuana,

00:17:48.310 --> 00:17:50.780
disorderly conduct and
public intoxication.

00:17:50.780 --> 00:17:52.570
If we use the standard legal definition

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:53.970
of misdemeanor in criminal law,

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.170
that would expose federal officials

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:58.050
to removal from very basic infractions.

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.150
And that's probably not what
the founding fathers want.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:02.950
The founding fathers
understood that misdemeanor

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:05.630
was a way to punish malrotation in office.

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:07.760
Which is an obsolete phrase,
but it basically means

00:18:07.760 --> 00:18:09.680
that it's corrupted behavior by a person

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:11.470
in a position of public trust.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:12.530
And not for nothing,

00:18:12.530 --> 00:18:15.210
in the 1868 impeachment of Andrew Johnson,

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.230
one of the improved
articles of impeachment

00:18:17.230 --> 00:18:20.837
was on numerous occasions
President Johnson quote made

00:18:20.837 --> 00:18:23.817
"With loud voice, certain
intemperate, inflammatory

00:18:23.817 --> 00:18:25.347
"and scandalous harangues

00:18:25.347 --> 00:18:28.697
"and did therein utter loud
threats and bitter menaces

00:18:28.697 --> 00:18:30.997
"against Congress and the
laws of the United States

00:18:30.997 --> 00:18:33.107
"duly enacted thereby amid the cries,

00:18:33.107 --> 00:18:35.130
"jeers and laughter of the multitudes

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:37.340
'then assembled and within bearing."

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:40.500
In other words, President
Johnson was impeached

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:43.120
for his purely political speech.

00:18:43.120 --> 00:18:45.300
Something that is absolutely not criminal

00:18:45.300 --> 00:18:47.200
in any way, shape or form.

00:18:47.200 --> 00:18:48.670
And there are plenty who believed

00:18:48.670 --> 00:18:50.140
that the president incited a riot.

00:18:50.140 --> 00:18:52.300
I think not in a technical, legal sense,

00:18:52.300 --> 00:18:54.250
but in a moral and ethical sense.

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:56.737
Like lawyer George Conway
who concluded quote,

00:18:56.737 --> 00:18:59.137
"Working together, Trump and
his family and his friends

00:18:59.137 --> 00:19:01.847
"use the rhetoric of
violence of armed uprising

00:19:01.847 --> 00:19:03.870
"and that is what they got."

00:19:03.870 --> 00:19:06.100
And I think Conway is probably
correct in the moral sense.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:09.340
This was a pro-Trump uprising
and it was predictable.

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:12.020
Since there were threats
posted all over social media.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:15.350
And the president's words
were at the very least

00:19:15.350 --> 00:19:17.750
unbelievably negligent and reckless

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:20.450
and might have even
been intended to provoke

00:19:20.450 --> 00:19:22.930
some sort of violent response.

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:24.680
It was certainly fomented, supported

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:26.420
and provoked by President Trump,

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:30.420
even if not legally incited
under the Brandenburg standard.

00:19:30.420 --> 00:19:31.810
But under that very standard,

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:33.100
we have to look at the meaning

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:34.850
of President Trump's actual words

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:38.010
and the actual likelihood
of provoking that violence.

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:39.150
But on the other hand,

00:19:39.150 --> 00:19:41.930
Congress can look at the
events of that entire day

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:45.020
and decide that President Trump
breached the public's trust.

00:19:45.020 --> 00:19:48.080
Not in a technical or legalistic sense,

00:19:48.080 --> 00:19:50.070
but in a political sense.

00:19:50.070 --> 00:19:53.650
Because not only was President
Trump enjoying the mobs siege

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:55.330
of the Capitol as it happened,

00:19:55.330 --> 00:19:57.550
but even as the mob turned violent,

00:19:57.550 --> 00:20:00.120
President Trump was busy
calling on Mike Pence

00:20:00.120 --> 00:20:02.150
and Senator Tuberville, and Senator Lee

00:20:02.150 --> 00:20:04.370
asking each one to be
sure to delay the vote

00:20:04.370 --> 00:20:06.190
to certify Biden's victory.

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:09.310
And it appears that when the
mayor of D.C asked for help

00:20:09.310 --> 00:20:12.600
President Trump refused to
send in the D.C National Guard.

00:20:12.600 --> 00:20:14.900
Apparently Maryland tried
to send help as well,

00:20:14.900 --> 00:20:16.320
but Governor Hogan said

00:20:16.320 --> 00:20:19.150
that the Pentagon inexplicably refused.

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:20.970
Between the president's acts

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:22.730
of actually contributing to the riot,

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:25.890
whether or not that meets the
legal standard of incitement

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:28.990
between his acts of trying
to make things worse,

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:30.580
to let the Capitol burn

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:33.680
and between his acts of
actually trying to literally

00:20:33.680 --> 00:20:35.600
subvert the democratic process

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:38.980
and prevent the certification
of his political rival

00:20:38.980 --> 00:20:41.640
as the next president of the United States

00:20:41.640 --> 00:20:45.100
those can contribute to
an impeachable offense.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.650
One that could lead to the
president being removed

00:20:47.650 --> 00:20:50.390
regardless of whether it meets any legal

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:52.300
or technical definition.

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:54.347
As one legal commentator put it,

00:20:54.347 --> 00:20:56.877
"Impeachment is a political
solution to a political problem.

00:20:56.877 --> 00:20:58.737
"It should be reserved
for truly heinous behavior

00:20:58.737 --> 00:21:00.137
"that harms the country.

00:21:00.137 --> 00:21:02.877
"But quote, this wasn't
technically a statutory crime

00:21:02.877 --> 00:21:06.410
"should play exactly zero
role in that analysis."

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:07.550
In other words,

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:10.823
if this is not impeachable
conduct, nothing is.

